`lib/App/karr/Git.pm:361-377` (fetch cannot delete) versus `:341-359` (force
push with prune).

`push` prunes correctly — verified, `karr delete 2` does remove the ref from
origin. But `pull` is a plain fetch and never deletes a local ref that the
remote dropped, so a stale clone re-creates it on its next write.

    A: karr delete 3 --yes         -> origin has 1 task ref
    B: karr sync                   -> "Done.", B still lists #3, and origin has #3 again
    A: karr sync --pull; karr list -> #3 is back

So `karr delete` is not durable in any multi-clone setup — the task returns as
soon as any other clone writes. Same family as the pull-refspec ticket: pull is
not the inverse of push. A fix for the refspec should cover deletions too
(prune on fetch), and the regression test should assert that a delete stays
deleted after a round trip through a second clone.
